Currently, the program creates the output video using OpenCV's VideoWriter. I want to design an alternative
  custom output format that takes into consideration that each pixel in the video contains 1 bit of data.
  Therefore a single byte of data can contain 8 pixels. This means that a frame of 640x480 pixels can be
  represented with 38400 bytes (unless I miscalculated). In additional to that, I would like to apply some
  sort of lossless compression on the data. I have been thinking of something like encoding long continuous
  ranges of single color with number instead of individual pixels. Also using Huffman coding has crossed my
  mind. But if you know of better alternatives, please feel free to suggest. You can also try to take
  advantage of diffs between subsequent frames, although I imagine that would be difficult for lossless
  videos. Come up with a detailed specification for such video file format. Design the file format to be
  lossless and as small as possible for 1-bit videos. Store the specification in doc/FORMAT.txt
